{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "04-safety-liveness.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Safety and liveness",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Tell \"nothing bad happens\" from \"something good eventually happens\" by the shape of their counterexamples, and see which promise from the scheduler makes \"eventually\" true.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A lock that never lets anyone in is perfectly safe: no state ever has both threads inside. Lesson 3's flags came close to that. So mutual exclusion is not all we want; we also want each thread that is waiting to get in, eventually.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The two claims fail differently. \"Never both inside\" fails at a moment: show the trace up to the bad state, and it is refuted, whatever comes after. \"Eventually gets in\" fails at no moment: after any trace that can go on, the thread might still get in later. A counterexample has to be a whole run that never gets there: one that stops, or one that goes on for ever.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Safety and liveness\n> A **safety** property says nothing bad ever happens; a finite trace refutes it (invariants are safety properties). A **liveness** property says something good eventually happens; no trace that can still go on refutes it, only a complete run that never gets there: one that stops in a **deadlock**, where no action is enabled, or, on a finite system, a **lasso**, a path into a loop that repeats for ever.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 3's flags gave a run of the first kind: both flags up, both threads waiting, no action enabled. Runs of the second kind need a closer look.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Runs that loop, and fairness",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Here nothing stops. Thread `q` waits to enter; thread `p` has work of its own, and alternates between working and resting.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Spin = system(\n  var p in {idle, busy}\n  var q in {wait, crit}\n  init p = idle ∧ q = wait\n  action pwork when p = idle do p := busy\n  action prest when p = busy do p := idle\n  action qenter when q = wait do q := crit\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Does `q` always get in eventually?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "eventually(Spin, q = crit)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "No: the lasso runs `p` for ever and never `q`, though `qenter` is enabled all along. That is not a bug in the threads; it is a scheduler that never runs `q`. Liveness in a concurrent system rests on a promise from the scheduler: a **fairness** assumption. **Weak fairness** for an action: if it stays enabled, it is eventually taken. A system marks such an action `fair`:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Spin2 = system(\n  var p in {idle, busy}\n  var q in {wait, crit}\n  init p = idle ∧ q = wait\n  action pwork when p = idle do p := busy\n  action prest when p = busy do p := idle\n  fair action qenter when q = wait do q := crit\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "eventually(Spin2, q = crit)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Weak fairness is not always enough. Put the lock back: `p` takes and releases it over and over, and `q` needs it free.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let W = system(\n  var p in {idle, crit}\n  var q in {wait, crit}\n  var lock in bool\n  init p = idle ∧ q = wait ∧ lock = false\n  action penter when p = idle ∧ lock = false do lock := true, p := crit\n  action pexit when p = crit do p := idle, lock := false\n  fair action qenter when q = wait ∧ lock = false do lock := true, q := crit\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "eventually(W, q = crit)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "On this lasso `qenter` is enabled only while `p` is out, never continuously, so weak fairness owes `q` nothing. **Strong fairness**: if an action is enabled again and again, it is eventually taken.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let S = system(\n  var p in {idle, crit}\n  var q in {wait, crit}\n  var lock in bool\n  init p = idle ∧ q = wait ∧ lock = false\n  action penter when p = idle ∧ lock = false do lock := true, p := crit\n  action pexit when p = crit do p := idle, lock := false\n  strong fair action qenter when q = wait ∧ lock = false do lock := true, q := crit\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "eventually(S, q = crit)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Peterson's algorithm needs only the weak promise, for every step. If `p` comes round again while `q` waits, its next move hands `q` the turn; from then on `q`'s way in stays open, and `p`'s is shut, until `q` has been through.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Peterson = system(\n  var p in {idle, flag, wait, crit}\n  var q in {idle, flag, wait, crit}\n  var fp in bool\n  var fq in bool\n  var turn in {P, Q}\n  init p = idle ∧ q = idle ∧ fp = false ∧ fq = false ∧ turn = P\n  fair action pflag when p = idle do fp := true, p := flag\n  fair action pyield when p = flag do turn := Q, p := wait\n  fair action penter when p = wait ∧ (fq = false ∨ turn = P) do p := crit\n  fair action pexit when p = crit do fp := false, p := idle\n  fair action qflag when q = idle do fq := true, q := flag\n  fair action qyield when q = flag do turn := P, q := wait\n  fair action qenter when q = wait ∧ (fp = false ∨ turn = Q) do q := crit\n  fair action qexit when q = crit do fq := false, q := idle\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "eventually(Peterson, q = crit)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Termination is liveness too: every run reaches a final state. Its proof is a measure, a natural number that every step lowers, because such a number cannot go down for ever.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem no_infinite_descent (f : Nat → Nat) (h : ∀ n, f (n + 1) < f n) : False := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that no sequence of natural numbers decreases forever.",
      "leanSolution": "  have key : ∀ n, f n + n ≤ f 0 := by\n    intro n\n    induction n with\n    | zero => simp\n    | succ k ih => have := h k; omega\n  have := key (f 0 + 1)\n  omega",
      "hints": [
        "Show first that $f(n) + n \\le f(0)$ for every $n$, by induction.",
        "Then take $n = f(0) + 1$: impossible."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Two workers each need two forks, and take them in opposite orders: `a` takes fork 1 first, `b` fork 2.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Forks = system(\n  var a in {idle, one, both}\n  var b in {idle, one, both}\n  var f1 in bool\n  var f2 in bool\n  init a = idle ∧ b = idle ∧ f1 = false ∧ f2 = false\n  action a1 when a = idle ∧ f1 = false do a := one, f1 := true\n  action a2 when a = one ∧ f2 = false do a := both, f2 := true\n  action arel when a = both do a := idle, f1 := false, f2 := false\n  action b1 when b = idle ∧ f2 = false do b := one, f2 := true\n  action b2 when b = one ∧ f1 = false do b := both, f1 := true\n  action brel when b = both do b := idle, f1 := false, f2 := false\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "deadlock(Forks)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Predict, then check: can `Forks` deadlock? Answer `true` or `false`.",
      "hints": [
        "Let each worker take its first fork.",
        "The usual cure is to take forks in one global order: with both taking fork 1 first, whoever holds it can always get fork 2."
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "eventually(Peterson, p = crit)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "With every step weakly fair, does `p` always get into `Peterson`'s critical section eventually?",
      "hints": [
        "The algorithm is symmetric."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Safety fails on a finite trace; liveness only on a complete run, one that stops (a deadlock) or one that loops (a lasso). Whether \"eventually\" holds depends on what the scheduler promises: weak fairness protects an action that stays enabled, strong fairness one that is enabled again and again.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Invariants, deadlocks, \"eventually\": three commands for three kinds of claim. The next lesson gives them one language, and one way to compute them all.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
